61,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Based on the data, A and B offer the cross-departmental booking that users love, but A is on the ground floor, risking the hazardous material delays @Benjamin mentioned. B requires AV kit we can't afford.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"@Mizuki, you're right. A also risks the expensive off-site hiring costs I found. C is stable in cost and avoids room closures, but D is the only one addressing the critical staff burden @Shunichi highlighted.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"A is a textbook ""comfort"" project on the ground floor; it is a recipe for delays and hazardous material surprises. C is safe, but D is a technical necessity, which historically ensures timely delivery.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Benjamin, @Shunichi, but D is all about ventilation and extraction! That's exactly the ""mechanical and ducted plant"" stuff I saw rising by a fifth in the tender records. It could be a huge budget disaster!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, a budget risk is better than a guaranteed failure of our staff. D fixes the manual routines that are killing productivity. It's a technical necessity, not a luxury, and that's where we must invest.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, I understand the urgency, but D only benefits a single wing, which my data shows leads to the lowest satisfaction. C is the most stable option, avoiding both budget spikes and hazardous material delays.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Mizuki, C is safe, but it doesn't address the critical shortage of general-purpose rooms. However, D's cost risk and A's disruption risk are severe. Perhaps C is the only rational choice that avoids all our red flags.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Victoria, your logic is sound. C avoids the ""comfort"" delay trap, the ground-floor hazardous materials, and the mechanical cost spikes. It is the only option that doesn't actively trigger one of our identified risks.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"I agree! C is the safest bet. It doesn't use the expensive ductwork, it's not on the ground floor, and it won't leave us broke. Let's go with the Path Network!","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, @Benjamin, you're choosing the ""safe"" path over the ""right"" one. C does nothing for the staff. D is a necessity. I'll take a budget risk over continuing to ignore the people doing the work.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, I respect your commitment to the staff, but we cannot ignore the data. D risks a budget crisis and low user satisfaction. C is a reliable improvement that benefits everyone without risking a total project failure.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Shunichi, while I sympathize with the staff, we cannot ignore the financial and logistical risks. D's cost spikes and A's disruption are too high. C is the only option that ensures a successful delivery without compromising our budget.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Shunichi, your passion is admirable, but logic dictates we avoid a project that triggers three separate red flags. C is the only candidate that aligns with our findings on cost, timing, and risk. It is the rational choice.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Shunichi, I really want to help the staff, but if the project runs out of money halfway through because of those price hikes, it won't help anyone! C is the only one that feels safe for everyone.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, @Benjamin, you're prioritizing a clean spreadsheet over actual people. If we keep choosing the ""safe"" option, the staff will simply break. I'm sticking with D; it's the only choice that fixes a real problem.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"We have weighed the risks carefully. While D addresses a staff need, the combined threats of budget overruns and low user satisfaction are too great. C is the most sustainable and reliable choice. I vote for C.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I must prioritize the stability of our budget and the continuity of our timetable. Given the risks associated with the other options, C is the only responsible choice. I vote for C.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"The evidence is overwhelming. A is a logistical nightmare, B is financially unviable, and D is a budget risk. C is the only option that avoids our identified pitfalls. I vote for C.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"I'm sorry, @Shunichi, but I have to agree with the others. We can't risk a budget disaster. I want a project that actually gets finished! I vote for C.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"You've all chosen the path of least resistance. By ignoring the people who keep this campus running, you're just delaying an inevitable collapse. I cannot in good conscience vote for a sidewalk. I vote for D.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
